-- Idempotent upsert for the 'How Explore Ranking Works' post into public.blogs.
-- Safe to run repeatedly (ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE). Use as a template for
-- future SQL-authored posts: keep 'body' as the jsonb block array the renderer reads.
insert into public.blogs (slug, title, excerpt, body, category, author, image, icons, read_mins, status, published_at)
values (
  'how-explore-ranking-works',
  'How Explore Ranking Works on Meeticulous: A Host’s Guide to Getting Found',
  'Your service’s spot on /explore isn’t random or pay-to-win — it’s a quality score computed from what you control. Here’s exactly what the algorithm measures, what moves it today, the penalties that quietly sink you, and a 30-minute setup that lifts most listings.',
  '[{"p":"When a guest opens the Explore page on Meeticulous, they don’t see hosts in a random order, and they don’t see whoever paid the most. They see services ranked by a quality score — a single number from 0 to 100 that the platform computes for every active service from signals you almost entirely control. This guide opens the box. You’ll learn exactly what the score measures, which levers actually move it today, the penalties that quietly drag good listings down, and a short setup routine that lifts most services in under half an hour."},{"p":"The important promise first: Explore ranking is earned, not bought. There is no bid, no boost, no “featured” fee that jumps you to the top of the main grid. Everything below is available to every host on every plan, for free. If you do the work, you rank."},{"h2":"How ranking works, in one sentence"},{"p":"Every active service gets a precomputed quality score (0–100); when a guest browses with no search, services are ordered by that score, and when a guest searches, the score is multiplied by how well your listing matches their words. That’s the whole model — quality × relevance."},{"p":"Two details matter. First, the score is precomputed, not calculated when a guest loads the page. It’s stored, refreshed when your data changes, and re-swept once a week. That’s why an edit you make today shows up in your ranking shortly after — not instantly on every keystroke, but close. Second, the score is per service, not per profile. If you offer three services, each is ranked on its own merits. A beautifully finished flagship service can rank well while a half-filled second listing sits far below it."},{"info":{"title":"The one-line mental model","text":"No search → services sorted by quality score, highest first. With a search → quality score × how closely your title, category, and description match the query. Quality is what you build; relevance is how well your words match what the guest typed."}},{"h2":"The five things the score measures"},{"p":"The 100 points are split across five areas. Knowing the weights tells you where your effort pays off most:"},{"table":{"head":["Area","Weight","What it rewards"],"rows":[["Customer Satisfaction","30","Ratings, review recency & count, repeat guests"],["Booking Performance","25","Recent bookings, completion rate"],["Reliability","20","Low cancellations, dependable delivery"],["Listing Quality & SEO","15","A complete, well-written, verified listing"],["Availability & Activity","10","Open slots, a fresh calendar, an active host"]]}},{"p":"On paper, Satisfaction and Bookings dominate — more than half the score. But here’s the honest part most ranking guides won’t tell you: those two areas are powered by history you accumulate over time. A brand-new service has no reviews and few bookings yet, so on day one the areas you can fully control — Listing Quality, Availability, and Reliability — decide almost everything. That’s good news. The parts you can fix in an afternoon are exactly the parts that get a new listing off the floor."},{"h2":"What actually moves your score today"},{"p":"Let’s separate the levers you can pull right now from the ones that build up over time. Pull the first list today; the second list rewards you for simply being good at your craft over the coming weeks."},{"p":"Fully in your control, effective immediately:"},{"ul":["A complete, well-written listing — title, description, cover image, price, category, FAQs.","An open, fresh calendar — real availability in the next 7 days, updated recently.","Being an active host — logging in and tending your page, not going dark.","Getting verified — completing KYC so your listing carries the trust signal.","Never cancelling on a guest — reliability is both scored and penalised."]},{"p":"Builds over time as you deliver great sessions:"},{"ul":["Reviews — their rating, how recent they are, and how many you’ve earned.","Repeat guests — people booking you more than once.","A steady flow of recent bookings.","A high completion rate — sessions that actually happen, not no-shows."]},{"info":{"title":"A note on honesty","text":"Some ranking signals you may have seen on other platforms — photo galleries, keyword tags, an “instant book” toggle, response-time scoring — are not part of Meeticulous’s scoring today. We’d rather tell you what actually works than pad this list. As the platform grows, this guide will grow with it."}},{"h2":"Listing Quality: the 30-minute win"},{"p":"This is the highest-return work for any host, because every point here is available immediately and none of it depends on history. Walk your listing through this checklist:"},{"steps":["Title — make it descriptive and specific, roughly 30 to 60 characters. “Strategy” scores nothing here; “90-Minute SEO Strategy Session for Founders” tells a guest (and the algorithm) exactly what they’re getting. Say the thing and, where it fits naturally, where you serve.","Description — write a real one, at least 150 characters, ideally far more. Explain who it’s for, what happens in the session, and what they walk away with. A thin description caps your listing-quality points and, worse, loses the guest.","Meta / SEO description — a clean 120–160 character summary. This is what shows in search results and previews; it’s also a scored field. One or two tight sentences.","Cover image — add one. A listing with a cover reads as finished; one without reads as abandoned. This is the single fastest visual credibility win.","Price — set one, and don’t hide it. A listed price (paid or free) scores; a hidden price does not. Guests trust a number.","Category — pick the right one. A correctly categorised service both scores and shows up when guests filter by profession.","FAQs — add at least three genuine questions and answers. This clears a scored threshold and pre-empts the questions that make guests hesitate.","Service area — set your location on your profile. It fills a scored field and helps local guests find you.","Get verified — complete KYC. A verified host carries a trust badge and a scored point; it’s also required before payouts, so there’s no reason to skip it."]},{"p":"None of this is clever. It’s just complete. The gap between a half-filled listing and a finished one is often the difference between page one and page three."},{"h2":"Availability & Activity: don’t go dark"},{"p":"Ten points ride on being genuinely bookable and genuinely present. The algorithm can tell the difference between a live host and a dormant one."},{"ul":["Keep real availability open in the next seven days — the more days with open slots, the better. A calendar with every day open scores the full band here.","Keep your calendar fresh — update it recently rather than letting it drift for weeks. A recently-touched calendar signals “I’m taking bookings.”","Stay active — log in and tend your page. A host who hasn’t appeared in weeks looks closed for business, and the score reflects that."]},{"p":"This area is small in points but outsized in consequence, because it also feeds the penalties below. An empty calendar doesn’t just cost you availability points — it triggers a multiplier that slashes your whole score."},{"h2":"The penalties that quietly sink good listings"},{"p":"Everything so far adds points. Penalties do something more dangerous: they multiply your entire score down, after all the adding is done. A great listing with a penalty attached can rank below a mediocre one without. There are three you need to respect."},{"info":{"title":"Zero availability — the score-killer","text":"If your service has no open availability, its score is multiplied by 0.3 — a 70% cut. This is the harshest penalty by far, and the most common self-inflicted wound. A superb listing with a closed calendar is a superb listing nobody can rank. Always keep real slots open."}},{"info":{"title":"Going inactive — the slow fade","text":"If you go inactive for more than 45 days, your score is multiplied by 0.6 — a 40% cut. Discovery rewards hosts who are actually here. A five-minute login and a calendar check every few weeks is enough to stay out of this band."}},{"info":{"title":"A recent cancellation — the trust dent","text":"A host-side cancellation in the last 7 days multiplies your score by 0.85. It’s the mildest of the three and it heals on its own after the week passes — but it’s a real reminder that cancelling on a guest costs you visibility, not just goodwill."}},{"p":"Read those together and the message is simple: stay open, stay active, and don’t cancel. Do those three things and you’re immune to the penalties that quietly bury most listings."},{"h2":"Reviews and bookings: the long game"},{"p":"More than half the total score lives in Satisfaction and Booking Performance, and you can’t fake either. They accrue from doing good work: guests book you, you deliver, they come back, they leave a five-star review. There’s no shortcut, and that’s deliberate — it keeps the top of Explore honest."},{"p":"What you can do is set the flywheel spinning. Every completed session is a booking-performance point and a chance at a review. Reviews are weighted on rating, how recent they are, and how many you’ve earned — so a steady trickle of fresh five-stars beats a burst that goes stale. Repeat guests count too, so a service worth booking twice compounds. Completion rate matters as much as volume: a booking that actually happens is worth far more than one that no-shows, so confirm, remind, and show up."},{"p":"If you’re brand new, don’t wait passively. Nail the levers in the earlier sections to earn your first page-one impressions, deliver so well that your first guests want to review you, and the heavy half of the score starts filling itself in."},{"h2":"How search relevance works"},{"p":"Everything above sets your quality score. When a guest types a query into Explore, a second factor kicks in: relevance. Your quality score is multiplied by how well your listing matches the words they used, and listings that don’t match at all are filtered out entirely."},{"p":"Matches are tiered by where the query appears:"},{"table":{"head":["Where the query matches","Relevance weight"],"rows":[["In your title","Strongest"],["In your category","Strong"],["In your description","Standard"],["Nowhere","Excluded from that search"]]}},{"p":"The practical takeaway: put the words your guests actually search into your title and category, in plain language. If people look for “career coaching,” a title that says “Career Coaching” will out-rank an equally-good service titled only “Consultation,” because relevance multiplies quality. Write for the words in a guest’s head, not the jargon in yours."},{"h2":"Common mistakes that cost hosts visibility"},{"ul":["A closed or empty calendar. The single most damaging mistake — it triggers the 0.3 multiplier and erases most of your score. Fix this before anything else.","A one-word or vague title. “Session,” “Call,” “Service” tell nobody anything and match no searches. Be specific.","A thin description. Under 150 characters caps your listing points and fails to convince the guest.","A hidden price. Guests skip what they can’t price, and the score rewards a listed price over a hidden one.","Skipping verification. Unverified means no trust badge, a lost point, and no payouts.","Going dark for weeks. Inactivity past 45 days costs 40% of your score for no reason other than absence.","Cancelling on guests. Beyond the immediate 7-day penalty, it suppresses the reviews and repeat bookings that carry the heavy half of the score.","Spreading effort thin across many half-built services instead of finishing one. Each service is ranked alone — one complete listing beats three empty ones."]},{"h2":"A tale of two services"},{"p":"Same profession, same skill, very different Explore outcomes — because of choices, not luck."},{"p":"Service A is titled “Consultation.” No cover image. A 40-character description. Price hidden. No FAQs. The host set up a calendar in March and hasn’t touched it since; there are no open slots this week. On paper the host is talented — but the listing scores near the floor on quality and then gets multiplied down by the zero-availability penalty. It sits on page three, and the host concludes “Explore doesn’t work.”"},{"p":"Service B is titled “90-Minute Career Strategy Session for Career Switchers.” It has a cover image, a 400-word description, a listed price, the right category, five FAQs, and a verified host. The calendar has open slots every day this week and was updated yesterday. It has two completed bookings and one recent five-star review. It ranks on page one, gets found, gets booked — and every new booking and review pushes it higher."},{"p":"The gap between them isn’t talent. It’s a finished listing, an open calendar, and showing up. Every bit of it is copyable in an afternoon."},{"h2":"Your 30-minute Explore setup"},{"steps":["Minutes 0–10 — Rewrite your title (specific, 30–60 chars) and description (150+ chars, ideally more). Add a meta description of 120–160 characters.","Minutes 10–18 — Add a cover image, set a visible price, confirm the correct category, and add at least three real FAQs.","Minutes 18–24 — Open real availability across the next seven days and save your calendar so it’s freshly dated. Set your service area on your profile.","Minutes 24–30 — Finish KYC verification if you haven’t. Then set a recurring reminder to log in every couple of weeks, keep the calendar open, and never let a cancellation or a 45-day silence creep in."]},{"p":"That’s it. You won’t out-rank a host with fifty five-star reviews on day one — but you’ll clear every penalty, max out every lever you control, and start earning the history that carries the rest."},{"h2":"Frequently asked questions"},{"p":"Can I pay to rank higher on Explore? No. The main Explore grid is ranked purely by quality score and search relevance. There’s no bid or boost that lifts you in it — which is exactly why doing the work pays off."},{"p":"How fast do my changes take effect? Quickly. Your score is recomputed when your data changes — for example when a booking or review comes in — and the whole catalogue is re-swept once a week, so time-based factors like calendar freshness and activity stay current. Make a good edit and your ranking follows shortly after."},{"p":"I’m brand new with no reviews. Am I stuck at the bottom? No. Reviews and booking history are a big part of the ceiling, but a new listing that nails listing quality, availability, reliability and verification clears every penalty and ranks on the strength of what it can control. That’s how you earn the first bookings that start the review flywheel."},{"p":"Why did my ranking drop? The usual culprits are a calendar that ran empty (the biggest penalty), a stretch of inactivity, or a recent cancellation. Check those three first — they’re almost always the cause, and all three are fixable."},{"p":"Does having more services help or hurt? Each service is ranked on its own. More services means more surface area in Explore, but only if each is genuinely complete. One finished, bookable service beats several half-filled ones every time."},{"p":"Do I have to keep my price visible? To rank best, yes — a listed price (paid or free) scores better than a hidden one, and guests trust a number they can see. Free is a perfectly good price; hidden is the one that costs you."},{"h2":"The bottom line"},{"p":"Explore ranking rewards exactly the things that make you a good host to book: a clear, complete listing; an open calendar; showing up; delivering; and earning reviews. There’s no trick, no fee, and no secret — just a score that adds up the quality you build and multiplies away the neglect you let slip in. Finish your listing, keep your calendar open, stay active, and let your delivered sessions do the compounding. Explore will meet you where you put in the work."},{"quote":"Ranking on Explore is earned, not bought. Finish the listing, open the calendar, show up — the score does the rest."}]'::jsonb,
  'Guide',
  'Meeticulous Team',
  NULL,
  '["chart","target","megaphone"]'::jsonb,
  16,
  'published',
  '2026-07-10T18:30:00.000Z'
)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title = excluded.title, excerpt = excluded.excerpt, body = excluded.body,
  category = excluded.category, author = excluded.author, image = excluded.image,
  icons = excluded.icons, read_mins = excluded.read_mins,
  status = excluded.status, published_at = excluded.published_at, updated_at = now();
